  <joint
    name="lidar_joint"
    type="fixed">
    <!-- z 1.1 -> 0.92 (2026-09-14): LiDAR measured 1.12 m above ground; base_joint
         already puts base_link 0.2 m up, so 1.1 double-counted it (1.30 m total)
         and flat ground read +0.19 m -> ground ring became costmap obstacles. -->
    <origin
      xyz="0.2925 0 0.92"
      rpy="0 0 1.5708" />
    <parent
      link="base_link" />
    <child
      link="lidar_link" />
    <axis
      xyz="0 0 0" />
  </joint>
  <!-- Intel RealSense D435, follow-me detector camera (added 2026-09-25).

       Measured by the user: lens centre 0.99 m above ground, aimed dead ahead
       with no pitch, mounted directly below the LiDAR. base_joint already lifts
       base_link 0.2 m, so z here is 0.99 - 0.2 = 0.79 (the LiDAR's 1.12 m is
       0.92 by the same arithmetic; the camera sits 0.13 m below it).

       x is taken from the LiDAR's 0.2925 since the camera is mounted under it,
       and y is 0. Of the three, y is the one that matters: a lateral error
       becomes a bearing error directly (0.05 m off centre = 1 deg at 3 m),
       while an error along x is almost purely radial and barely moves the
       bearing at all.

       The child link name must stay d435_link: the driver derives its own frame
       prefix from the camera_name parameter (parameters.cpp:83) and publishes
       d435_link -> d435_color_optical_frame itself, so this joint is what
       connects that subtree to the robot. Without it a detection bearing cannot
       be transformed into the LiDAR's frame at all.

       The D435's own camera_link sits at the depth (left) imager rather than the
       physical lens centre, roughly 1-2 cm away. At 3 m that is under half a
       degree, well inside what the bearing needs.

       ROLL IS PI: the camera is mounted UPSIDE DOWN (confirmed 2026-09-25 from a
       live frame; the scene is only upright after a 180 degree rotation). This
       is not cosmetic. With roll 0 the optical axes would be mirrored, so a
       person on the rover's left would be computed as being on its right and
       the follow controller would turn AWAY from them. The image itself is
       rotated separately, inside the detector, because YOLOX is not rotation
       invariant; that rotation is for inference only and detections are mapped
       back to raw pixels so this transform stays the single source of truth. -->
  <link name="d435_link">
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <geometry>
        <box size="0.025 0.090 0.025" />
      </geometry>
      <material name="">
        <color rgba="0.1 0.1 0.1 1" />
      </material>
    </visual>
  </link>
  <joint
    name="d435_joint"
    type="fixed">
    <origin
      xyz="0.2925 0 0.79"
      rpy="3.14159265 0 0" />
    <parent
      link="base_link" />
    <child
      link="d435_link" />
    <axis
      xyz="0 0 0" />
  </joint>
